WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:08.000
Last video was a big long rundown of the various editing problems that plagued Warner Brothers Film "Suicide Squad" and the comments got... um,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:15.030
Th-there's a lot of them. First thing: a correction. Fox is the studio responsible for Fan-4-Stic, not Sony.

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:20.579
This was purely an error on my part. I-I got a brain cloud, though in my defense

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:25.440
I ran the script by a number of people and none of them caught it either because it's just

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.680
that believable that Sony would also produce something like Fan-4-Stic.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:38.120
To be clear, Fox holds the film rights for Fantastic Four, X-Men, and Deadpool, while Sony holds the rights to Spider-Man, which...

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.180
You know, hasn't exactly been stellar these days either.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.260
Second: the fate of Griggs.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:49.500
In the video, I highlight that the chief security guard, Griggs, is overdeveloped in the front half the movie,

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.000
but disappears after this interaction with Harley as the squatter being loaded onto a plane for Midway city.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.480
A lot of people popped into the comments to mention that

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.180
Griggs is seen again being tortured by Joker

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:06.500
and this is to me actually a great illustration of how bad the overall structure of the movie is that so many people would,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.240
in retrospect, be unable to accurately place the scene order.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.640
So the scene that people are referring to is this one: where Joker threatens Griggs

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.980
And it cuts away before we find out what Joker wants or plans.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:21.200
But this scene actually comes much earlier.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.060
In fact, it's way back before Enchantress even breaks her brother out.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:31.000
In that scene Griggs presumably gets the cell phone that he later gives to Harley, and then after that,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.300
he's never seen again.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:39.900
Now a few people suggested that he's in the helicopter when Harley escapes, working the minigun. But that's just another one of Joker's henchmen.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.720
So Griggs is seen interacting with the inmates during all of the repetitive introductions,

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:50.880
then he goes to the casino where he's intercepted by Joker's gang, and finally he gives the cell to Harley and

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.880
Then he's never seen again.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.700
Third, another small correction.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:02:00.120
Trailer Park is the company that Warner Brothers reportedly got to do an alternate competing cut of the movie,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:09.020
However, their prior involvement was actually the 2015 comic con sneak preview and not the bohemian Rhapsody trailer like I said in the video

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:13.560
That trailer, the theatrical trailer, and all the TV spots were cut by another company:

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.300
Aspect.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:21.040
So the confusion is the trailer park was brought in to make a full cut more along the lines of the trailers that were cut by Aspect.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.160
Fourth: the bottle was cough syrup, and it was full of water

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.220
Or Was It?!

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:28.280
It was water.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.840
Lastly a note on the difference between structural editing problems and script problems

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:37.619
How do you tell if something is poorly edited or if it was forced that way by poor writing?

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.769
This is a legitimately difficult thing to reverse engineer out of a finished film unless you have access to the shooting script

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.420
So for the purpose of analysis, it's an editing problem if the material exists, but is in the wrong order

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:54.360
And it's a scripting problem if the material doesn't exist at all.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:00.560
so for example: the Flash cameo sequence in Batman V Superman is ultimately pointless and confusing,

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.040
focusing on oblique franchise building rather than any sort of character or plot development,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.660
relative to the actual movie.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.140
So while that's a script problem because they wrote it that way

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:16.720
It's more pertinently an editing problem because the editor should have dropped it. These are interlocking problems though

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:24.180
Suicide squads poor pace is a direct consequence of poor writing because all the characters were written in their compartmentalized bits

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.000
It's really hard for the editors to make that move at a good pace

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.200
To reiterate from the longer Breakdown: a more coherent cut of the film almost certainly exists

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:39.160
But it would be an incredibly long, slow-moving, cut of the film as well with even more time dedicated to

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:44.440
Backstories and time in prison, something that already takes up almost half of the runtime.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:45.920
And that's it for this week.

